---
import TopicPage from '../../../../components/TopicPage.astro';
import Widget from '../../../../components/Widget.astro';

const statCfg = {
  steps: [
    { math: '1 · Every estimate has a distribution', note: 'Sample means wobble — the standard error quantifies it. Quoting "conversion = 3.2%" without its interval is quoting noise with confidence.' },
    { math: '2 · Sampling beats formulas sometimes', note: 'The bootstrap resamples your data to derive uncertainty for ANY statistic — medians, 90th percentiles, "share of users with 3+ sessions" — where no closed-form formula exists.' },
    { math: '3 · Know the three workhorses', note: 'Regression (relationships), hypothesis testing (comparisons), Bayesian updating (belief revision). The statistics ladder covers each in full; data science uses them daily.' },
    { math: '4 · Effect size before significance', note: 'p < 0.05 with a tiny effect on a huge sample is usually trivial. Report the size and interval — statistically significant is not the same as matters.' },
  ],
};

const statQuizCfg = {
  questions: [
    { q: 'A conversion rate of 3.2% measured on 400 users has a 95% interval of roughly…', choices: ['3.0% to 3.4%', '1.5% to 4.9%', '2.2% to 4.2%', '0% to 100%'], answer: 1, why: 'SE = √(0.032 × 0.968 / 400) ≈ 0.0088, so the interval is 3.2% ± 1.96 × 0.88% ≈ (1.5%, 4.9%). Small samples make intervals embarrassingly wide — that is the point.' },
    { q: 'The mean of a dataset is the value that minimises…', choices: ['the sum of absolute deviations', 'the sum of squared deviations', 'the range', 'the number of data points above it'], answer: 1, why: 'Squaring gives the mean its special status: it is the least-squares centre, which links it to regression, to the standard deviation, and to why outliers drag it.' },
    { q: 'A p-value of 0.03 means…', choices: ['there is a 3% chance the null hypothesis is true', 'the effect is 3% large', 'if the null hypothesis were true, data this extreme would appear about 3% of the time', 'the result is 97% certain to replicate'], answer: 2, why: 'p-values are conditional on the null being true — they measure how surprising the data would be, not the probability of any hypothesis. Confusing the two is the most common statistical error in industry.' },
    { q: 'The bootstrap estimates uncertainty by…', choices: ['collecting more samples from the population', 'resampling the observed data with replacement and recomputing the statistic', 'assuming a normal distribution always', 'removing outliers'], answer: 1, why: 'The sample stands in for the population; thousands of resamples trace out the sampling distribution of any statistic — median, percentile, whatever — with no formula required.' },
  ],
};

const statEngineMatch = {
  "prompt": "Pair each artefact of data-science work with the statistics underneath it:",
  "pairs": [
    {
      "left": "a machine-learning model",
      "right": "regression and classification at scale"
    },
    {
      "left": "an A/B test",
      "right": "hypothesis testing with a dashboard attached"
    },
    {
      "left": "anomaly detection",
      "right": "distribution thinking"
    },
    {
      "left": "a '3.2% conversion' on a slide",
      "right": "a sample statistic wearing confidence it has not earned"
    }
  ],
  "why": "The machinery is already owned — sampling distributions, the central limit theorem, Bayes' rule — organised around the four questions a working data scientist asks."
};

const statSDest = {
  "prompt": "Session counts 4, 6 and 8 have a mean of 6. Roughly what is their standard deviation?",
  "unit": "sessions",
  "answer": 1.63,
  "factor": 1.4,
  "why": "√(8/3) ≈ 1.63 — the root-mean-square of the deviations, which is the hinge connecting the mean to least-squares regression and the whole linear-algebra machinery of machine learning.",
  "context": "the deviations are −2, 0 and +2; squared, they sum to 8"
};

const statEmpPlace = {
  "prompt": "For bell-shaped data, roughly what share of values lies within 2 standard deviations of the mean?",
  "labels": [
    "50%",
    "68%",
    "80%",
    "95%",
    "99.7%"
  ],
  "scale": {
    "min": 50,
    "max": 99.7
  },
  "answer": 95,
  "tolerance": 3,
  "why": "About 95% within 2 SDs, 99.7% within 3 — while Chebyshev guarantees only 1 − 1/k² for any shape: at least 89% within 3 SDs, whatever the distribution."
};

const statSqrtNPredict = {
  "q": "Your confidence interval is twice as wide as the business can act on. What must you do to the sample size?",
  "choices": [
    "Quadruple it — the standard error shrinks with the square root of n",
    "Double it — error scales one-to-one with sample size",
    "Nothing — intervals shrink on their own as the metric matures"
  ],
  "answer": 0,
  "why": "SE shrinks with √n, so halving the error takes four times the sample — the single most expensive square root in industry."
};

const statCourtCloze = {
  "text": "Hypothesis testing as a court procedure: the %%0%% hypothesis is the boring defendant — nothing changed, the difference is luck; the %%1%% statistic measures how far the data sit from boring; and the %%2%%-value asks how often chance alone would produce data at least this extreme.",
  "answers": [
    [
      "null"
    ],
    [
      "test"
    ],
    [
      "p",
      "P"
    ]
  ],
  "hint": "The defendant, the measuring stick, and the surprise level."
};

const statHackEst = {
  "prompt": "Testing 20 independent metrics at threshold 0.05 under a true null — what is the chance at least one looks 'significant'?",
  "unit": "%",
  "answer": 64,
  "factor": 1.3,
  "why": "1 − 0.95²⁰ ≈ 64% — decide the threshold and the analysis before looking, or the winner is mostly luck wearing a p-value.",
  "context": "one pre-declared test keeps the false-positive rate at its promised 5%"
};

const statPermCompare = {
  "prompt": "Two ways to compare two groups — drag the divider:",
  "before": {
    "label": "classical",
    "text": "compare the difference in means against its standard error — formulas and normality assumptions"
  },
  "after": {
    "label": "permutation",
    "text": "shuffle the group labels at random, recompute the difference, and score your result against what chance alone produces"
  },
  "why": "No normality assumption, no formula for the statistic — the permutation test works for means, medians, conversion rates, anything computable."
};

const statKidneyBranch = {
  "root": "Kidney-stone study, 1986: open surgery (A) succeeded on 78% of patients; the minimally invasive procedure (B) on 83%. Which treatment?",
  "nodes": {
    "start": {
      "text": "Before deciding — what do you check?",
      "choices": [
        {
          "label": "Split by stone size",
          "next": "flip",
          "feedback": "Small stones: A 93% vs 87%. Large stones: A 73% vs 69%. A wins both subgroups — B's headline was inflated by its easier patient mix."
        },
        {
          "label": "Trust the aggregate — it has more patients",
          "next": "confound",
          "feedback": "The aggregate was confounded: doctors reserved open surgery for the hardest cases, so the pooled comparison compares different patient mixes."
        },
        {
          "label": "Randomise the next cohort of patients",
          "next": "design",
          "feedback": "Random assignment breaks the link between treatment and case difficulty — the design-level fix arithmetic cannot supply."
        }
      ]
    },
    "flip": {
      "text": "Treatment A wins both subgroups and loses the aggregate — Simpson's paradox in the data.",
      "choices": [
        {
          "label": "So what repairs it?",
          "next": "design",
          "feedback": "Only design: randomised assignment, so the groups are comparable before anything is aggregated."
        }
      ]
    },
    "confound": {
      "text": "No amount of careful arithmetic on confounded groups repairs the confounding."
    },
    "design": {
      "text": "Aggregation is a modelling choice that can reverse a conclusion — the experimental-design chapter exists because of this table."
    }
  },
  "why": "Simpson's paradox is the statistical argument for randomised design: the aggregate can be the average of several opposite stories."
};

const statTailSpot = {
  "prompt": "One of these percentile claims is backwards — click it:",
  "lines": [
    "p50 latency describes the typical user's experience.",
    "p95 and p99 describe the experiences a support team will hear about.",
    "The gap between mean and p99 is a direct read of tail weight.",
    "Dashboards that show only means reveal the tail best."
  ],
  "errorLine": 3,
  "correction": "Dashboards that show only means systematically hide the tail — and the tail is where the incidents live.",
  "why": "Report percentiles alongside means for skewed operational data; the mean alone is the tail's accomplice."
};
---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/data-science/advanced/statistics-for-data-science">
  <h2>Where you are, and what you will learn</h2>
  <p>
    This is an advanced Data Science lesson. It assumes the foundational <em>From Data to
    Decisions</em> — dataset, question, claim — and the intermediate rungs where you learned
    to prepare, query and look at data: <em>Cleaning Messy Data</em>, <em>SQL for
    Analysts</em>, <em>Exploratory Data Analysis</em> and <em>Charts That Tell the
    Truth</em>. This chapter adds the inference layer the working analyst needs on top of
    all that: how much an estimate wobbles, whether a difference is real or luck, and which
    of two groups is better.
  </p>
  <p>By the end you will be able to:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>derive the mean and median from the loss functions they minimise, and choose between them by the shape of the data;</li>
    <li>attach a standard error and a confidence interval to a proportion, and explain the square-root-of-n cost of precision;</li>
    <li>bootstrap an interval for statistics with no closed-form formula;</li>
    <li>define a p-value correctly — and simulate one rather than memorise a formula;</li>
    <li>compare two groups with a permutation test and recognise Simpson's paradox before it ambushes an aggregate.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Data science runs on statistics whether or not it admits it. A machine-learning model is
    regression and classification at scale; an A/B test is hypothesis testing with a dashboard
    attached; anomaly detection is distribution thinking; every "3.2% conversion" on a slide is
    a sample statistic wearing a suit of confidence it has not earned. You already own the
    machinery from the statistics ladder — sampling distributions, the central limit theorem,
    Bayes' rule. This chapter organises it around the four questions a working data scientist
    actually asks, in the order they arrive: <strong>what is typical</strong> (and why the mean
    earns its job), <strong>how uncertain is my estimate</strong>, <strong>is this difference
    real or luck</strong>, and <strong>which of two groups is better</strong>.
  </p>
  <Widget no="11" caption="pair each daily artefact with its statistical engine" widget="match" config={statEngineMatch} />

  <Widget no="1" caption="the four statistical habits of effective data scientists" widget="equationStep" config={statCfg} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> the four habits form a working order, not a syllabus —
    every estimate wears an interval, the bootstrap covers the statistics no formula
    reaches, the three workhorses divide the daily questions between them, and effect size
    is reported before significance. Read the notes as a checklist for any number you are
    about to put on a slide: each step catches a different way of quoting noise with
    confidence.
  </p>

  <h2>Why the mean matters — and when it lies</h2>
  <p>
    The mean is not arbitrary; it is the <strong>balance point</strong> of the data, and more
    precisely it is the one number that minimises the sum of <em>squared</em> deviations. Take
    session counts 4, 6 and 8: the mean is 6, the deviations are −2, 0 and +2, and no other
    centre produces a smaller total of squared errors (try 5: 1 + 1 + 9 = 11, against 4 + 0 +
    4 = 8 at the mean). That least-squares property is not a curiosity — it is the hinge
    connecting the mean to the <strong>standard deviation</strong>, which is itself the
    root-mean-square of the deviations (here √(8/3) ≈ 1.63), to least-squares regression, and
    to the entire linear-algebra machinery of machine learning. The median, by contrast,
    minimises the sum of <em>absolute</em> deviations — a different loss function, a different
    centre. Choose the loss, and the summary statistic chooses itself; that is why income data
    gets reported with medians (one billionaire moves the mean, not the median) and why
    "average" without a qualifier is an incomplete sentence.
  </p>
  <Widget no="10" caption="call the spread of the three sessions" widget="estimate" config={statSDest} />
  <p>
    Spread is the other half of the story. The standard deviation is comparable across
    datasets in a way the raw range never is, because the range grows with sample size while
    the SD stabilises. A useful worst-case bound: for <em>any</em> distribution, Chebyshev's
    inequality guarantees that at least 1 − 1/k² of the data lies within k standard deviations
    of the mean — so at least 89% within 3 SDs, whatever the shape. For bell-shaped data the
    empirical rule is far tighter: about 68% within 1 SD, 95% within 2, 99.7% within 3. When a
    metric suddenly lives 4 SDs from its usual value, either something remarkable happened or
    something broke — both are worth a page of investigation.
  </p>
  <Widget no="9" caption="drop the marker — the two-SD share" widget="place" config={statEmpPlace} />
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> the mean is "the normal value" of a dataset. It is only a
    centre of mass, and it can sit where no data point sits. For the waiting times 1, 2, 3,
    4 and 90 minutes, the mean is 20 minutes — a value between observations 4 and 5 that no
    customer actually experienced. Report the mean <em>and</em> the median, and the gap
    between them is itself information about skew.
  </p>

  <p><strong>So far:</strong> the mean earns its job by minimising squared deviations and the median by absolute ones — choose the loss, and the centre chooses itself — while spread is the other half of every summary. The next step is the first professional reflex: no point estimate leaves the room without its uncertainty.</p>

  <h2>Estimation: point estimates, standard errors and the bootstrap</h2>
  <p>
    Every metric on a dashboard is an estimate from a sample, and the first professional
    reflex is to attach its <strong>standard error</strong>. For a proportion p̂ from n users,
    SE = √(p̂(1 − p̂)/n). The worked case from the widget: conversion 3.2% on 400 users gives
    SE = √(0.032 × 0.968 / 400) ≈ 0.0088, so a 95% confidence interval is 3.2% ± 1.96 × 0.88%,
    roughly (1.5%, 4.9%) — a band nearly three points wide, on a metric quoted to a tenth of a
    point. The same construction with n = 40,000 narrows the interval to (2.8%, 3.6%). Note
    the cruel arithmetic: to halve the error you must <em>quadruple</em> the sample, because
    SE shrinks with √n. That square root is the single most expensive square root in industry.
  </p>
  <Widget no="8" caption="bet before you read on — the price of precision" widget="predict" config={statSqrtNPredict} />
  <p>
    Many statistics have no tidy formula — the median revenue per user, the 90th percentile of
    latency, "the share of sessions that last over 10 minutes". The <strong>bootstrap</strong>
    solves all of them at once: resample your own n data points <em>with replacement</em>,
    compute the statistic, repeat 10,000 times, and read the 2.5th and 97.5th percentiles of
    the resulting distribution as the interval. The logic is the plug-in principle: the sample
    is to the population as the bootstrap sample is to the sample. It is not magic — it fails
    for extreme order statistics and tiny samples — but it turns uncertainty measurement from
    an exam question into a loop you can write in six lines, which is why it quietly powers
    much of modern practice.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> a 95% confidence interval means "there is a 95% probability
    that the true value is in this interval". The correct reading is about the
    <em>procedure</em>: if you repeated the sampling and interval-building forever, 95% of the
    intervals would capture the truth. The true value is fixed; the interval is the thing that
    wobbles. (Bayesian credible intervals do support the probability reading — but only after
    you have committed to a prior, which is a modelling decision, not a free lunch.)
  </p>

  <p><strong>So far:</strong> every dashboard number is a sample statistic, the standard error prices its wobble, and the bootstrap manufactures intervals where no closed form exists. The question changes next: not how big is the estimate, but could luck alone have produced it?</p>

  <h2>Testing hypotheses: the null, the statistic, the p-value</h2>
  <p>
    Hypothesis testing is a court procedure for data. The <strong>null hypothesis</strong> is
    the boring defendant — "nothing changed, the difference is luck"; the
    <strong>test statistic</strong> measures how far the data sit from boring; the
    <strong>p-value</strong> asks: if the null were true, how often would chance alone produce
    data at least this extreme? A small p-value is not proof of truth; it is a statement that
    the boring world struggles to explain what you saw. The cleanest version needs no
    formulas: <strong>simulation</strong>. Suppose a new checkout page shows 60 conversions in
    100 visits against a baseline of 50%. Under the null, simulate 100 coin flips thousands
    of times, count how often 60 or more heads appear. That frequency <em>is</em> the p-value
    (here roughly 2.8%), and because you built it yourself, the definition — "probability of
    data this extreme <em>given the null</em>" — stops being a paragraph and becomes a thing
    you have watched happen.
  </p>
  <Widget no="7" caption="fill the blanks — the court's three roles" widget="cloze" config={statCourtCloze} />
  <p>
    Three usage rules keep testing honest. First, decide the threshold (usually 0.05) and the
    analysis <em>before</em> looking — testing twenty metrics and reporting the winner is
    p-hacking, and with 20 independent tests the chance of at least one "significant" result
    under a true null is 1 − 0.95²⁰ ≈ 64%. Second, a p-value says nothing about importance:
    with n = 2 million, a 0.01-point lift can be significant and worthless. Third, absence of
    significance is not evidence of absence — a wide interval containing zero may merely mean
    the experiment was too small to see the effect you care about.
  </p>
  <Widget no="6" caption="call the p-hacker's false-winner odds" widget="estimate" config={statHackEst} />

  <p><strong>So far:</strong> the p-value measures how surprising the data would be if nothing had changed — and it keeps that meaning only when the threshold and the analysis are fixed before looking. Applied to two groups at once, the same logic gets sharper and grows a famous trap.</p>

  <h2>Comparing two samples: A/B logic and the permutation test</h2>
  <p>
    "Which group is better?" is the two-sample generalisation of testing. The classical route
    compares the difference in means against its standard error; the modern, more general
    route is the <strong>permutation test</strong>: pool both groups' data, then repeatedly
    shuffle the group labels at random and recompute the difference in means. Under the null
    — that group membership is irrelevant — the shuffles generate the full distribution of
    differences chance could produce, and your observed difference is scored against it. No
    normality assumption, no formula for the statistic, works for means, medians, conversion
    rates, anything computable. Pair it with a <strong>confidence interval for the
    difference</strong> (bootstrap the two groups separately), and you have the complete
    professional answer: "variant B wins by 1.1 points (95% CI 0.3 to 1.9, permutation
    p = 0.008)".
  </p>
  <Widget no="5" caption="the classical route versus the permutation test — drag the divider" widget="compare" config={statPermCompare} />
  <p>
    The deepest warning about two-sample comparisons has a name and a canonical
    dataset. In a 1986 clinical study of kidney-stone treatments, open surgery (A)
    succeeded on 78% of patients and the minimally invasive procedure (B) on 83% — so
    B looks better. Split by stone size and the verdict flips twice: for small stones
    A succeeded 93% to 87%; for large stones A succeeded 73% to 69%. Treatment A wins
    both subgroups and loses the aggregate, because assignment was not randomised:
    doctors reserved open surgery for the hardest cases, and B's headline was inflated
    by its easier patient mix. This is <strong>Simpson's paradox</strong>, and it is
    the statistical argument for the experimental-design chapter next door:
    aggregation is a modelling choice that can reverse a conclusion, and no amount of
    careful arithmetic on confounded groups repairs the confounding.
  </p>
  <Widget no="4" caption="the 78% versus 83% decision" widget="branch" config={statKidneyBranch} />
  <p>
    A companion habit: for skewed operational data, report <strong>percentiles
    alongside means</strong>. The p50 latency describes the typical user's experience;
    p95 and p99 describe the experiences a support team will hear about — and the gap
    between mean and p99 is a direct read of tail weight. Dashboards that show only
    means systematically hide the tail, and the tail is where the incidents live.
  </p>
  <Widget no="3" caption="find the backwards claim — reading the tail" widget="spot-error" config={statTailSpot} />
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> a significant two-sample difference means the treatment
    <em>caused</em> the improvement. Statistics never assigns cause — design does. Only
    random assignment (as in a true A/B test) breaks the link between group membership and
    pre-existing differences; compare power users against casual ones and every difference is
    confounded with who they already were. The lesson's sibling on A/B testing works through
    the design side in full.
  </p>

  <h2>Check yourself</h2>
  <p>Try these before moving on. Feedback follows each answer.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>A dataset of waiting times reads 1, 2, 3, 4 and 90 minutes. Which centre would you report, and what does the gap between mean and median tell you?</li>
    <li>A conversion rate of 3.2% is measured on 400 users. Give the interval, and explain why quoting "3.2%" alone is malpractice.</li>
    <li>In the kidney-stone study, treatment A beat treatment B in both size subgroups yet lost in the aggregate. What does that prove about aggregates, and which design flaw made it possible?</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    <strong>Feedback:</strong> (1) The median: 3 minutes, against a mean of 20 that no
    customer actually experienced. The gap signals heavy right skew — report both, and the
    distance between them is a direct read of the tail's weight. (2) The standard error is
    about 0.88 points, so the 95% interval runs roughly from 1.5% to 4.9% — a band nearly
    three points wide on a metric quoted to a tenth of a point; and halving that interval
    would take 1,600 users, not 400, because precision improves with the square root of n.
    (3) Aggregation is a modelling choice that can reverse a conclusion. Non-random
    assignment sent the hardest cases to open surgery, so the pooled comparison was
    confounded — and no arithmetic on the aggregated table repairs the confounding. Only
    the design does, which is why the sibling lesson on A/B testing works the experimental
    side in full.
  </p>

  <h2>Key takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>The mean minimises squared error, the median absolute error — choose the loss, and the centre chooses itself.</li>
    <li>Every estimate needs its standard error; precision scales with the square root of n.</li>
    <li>The bootstrap turns uncertainty into a loop: resample with replacement, recompute, read the percentiles.</li>
    <li>A p-value is the surprise level of the data under the null — nothing more, nothing less.</li>
    <li>Fix thresholds and analyses before looking; twenty tests at 0.05 hand you a false winner about 64% of the time.</li>
    <li>Report percentiles alongside means for skewed data — the tail is where the incidents live.</li>
    <li>Statistics never assigns cause; only design — random assignment — does.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Where this goes next</h2>
  <p>
    The sibling advanced lessons put this machinery to work: <em>A/B Testing</em> is the
    experimental-design side of two-sample comparison, <em>Dashboards &amp; Data
    Storytelling</em> is where intervals and percentiles must survive a five-second read,
    and <em>Machine Learning Basics</em> scales regression and classification — the same
    least-squares core this chapter attached to the mean.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Why the central limit theorem lets all this work.</strong> The CLT says the
    distribution of a sample mean approaches normal as n grows, whatever the population's
    shape, with standard error σ/√n. This is why one formula covers conversion rates, session
    times and revenue. But read the fine print: the theorem needs finite variance (wealth
    distributions can violate it), enough n (how much depends on skew — for heavily skewed
    revenue data, n in the thousands), and independence (blocked or networked users violate
    it). When populations are heavy-tailed, medians and trimmed means beat means, and the
    bootstrap interval for the mean can be badly biased. Knowing when the CLT's comfort
    blanket fails is a genuine mark of seniority.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>The Bayesian alternative.</strong> Frequentist testing asks "how surprising is
    this data under the null?"; Bayesian updating asks "how should my beliefs change?",
    treating the unknown parameter as a distribution to be revised: posterior ∝ likelihood ×
    prior. For A/B decisions the Bayesian framing gives the quantity business stakeholders
    actually want — "probability B beats A is 97%" — without the p-value's inverted logic.
    The costs are real: priors inject judgement, sequential updating changes error
    guarantees, and communication ("what's a posterior?") is its own project. Neither school
    is correct; they answer different questions, and strong practitioners keep both in the
    kit.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Open problems of scale.</strong> Modern data pipelines break classical
    assumptions in new directions: metrics are computed on overlapping windows (dependence),
    experiments run on networks where treated users influence controls (interference), and
    dashboards test hundreds of metrics daily (multiplicity at industrial scale — hence
    false-discovery-rate control). The replication crisis in science and the "A/B testing
    industrial complex" in tech are the same phenomenon: inference machinery used as a
    ratchet rather than as a truth-finder. The statistics is old; the discipline of applying
    it at scale is the live research frontier.
  </p>

  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Wasserman, <em>All of Statistics</em>; Efron &amp; Hastie, <em>Computer Age Statistical Inference</em>; Adhikari, DeNero &amp; Wagner, <em>Computational and Inferential Thinking</em> (Data 8); Efron &amp; Tibshirani, <em>An Introduction to the Bootstrap</em>; the statistics ladder of this atlas. Source text: OpenStax (https://openstax.org), CC BY 4.0 — adapted for Interactive Atlas.</p>
  <Widget no="2" caption="check yourself: intervals, means and p-values" widget="quiz" config={statQuizCfg} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> the four answers share one theme — precision about what
    a statistical statement actually claims. An interval is a procedure, the mean is a
    least-squares centre, a p-value is conditional on the null, and the bootstrap is the
    sample standing in for the population. Each wrong choice is the statement people wish
    the statistic made; the right ones are what it actually says.
  </p>
</TopicPage>
